## 3. Strengths

1. **Excellent architecture doc fidelity** — the implementation faithfully mirrors every section of the architecture spec, with code comments citing specific sections
2. **Incident-centered design** — the entire system is genuinely built around the `Incident` model; every signal, every ML output, every LLM call feeds into it
3. **Noisy-OR signal fusion** in the Threat Correlation Engine — a principled choice that compounds independent evidence without artificial ceilings
4. **Graceful degradation everywhere** — DistilBERT falls back to heuristics, Gemini falls back to templates, RAG falls back to MITRE-overlap search, threat intel degrades per-provider
5. **Clean layered architecture** — Repository → Service → API separation with domain exceptions translated at a single middleware layer
6. **Strong typing throughout** — Python type hints + Pydantic schemas + TypeScript strict mode + shared types
7. **Good test infrastructure** — 13 unit tests + 9 integration tests with an elegant `requires_database` skip-if-unavailable pattern
8. **Production-quality middleware** — rate limiting, structured JSON/human logging, centralized error handling with request IDs
9. **Sensible ML pipeline** — feature engineering is shared between training and inference, model loading is lazy+singleton+thread-safe
10. **Well-documented** — 7 markdown docs covering architecture, API, installation, deployment, developer guide, testing, and folder structure
11. **Extension security** — content script is dependency-free (IIFE), auth tokens stored in `chrome.storage.local`, MV3-compliant

